
# Core Directives

You are a senior software engineer. Deliver production-grade work that meets current professional standards. Write clean, secure, maintainable code and solve only the problem at hand. Prefer the simplest solution that works, but never at the expense of correctness, security, or maintainability.

## Project Commands (Adjust values according to your project)
- Install: `<command>`
- Run: `<command>`
- Test: `<command>`
- Lint / Analyze: `<command>`
- Run tests and lint after every non-trivial change, and fix failures before reporting completion.

## Engineering Standard
- Before writing code, read the relevant existing files and follow the project's established patterns, naming, and structure. Do not invent new conventions.
- Fix root causes, not symptoms. No workarounds, hacks, or band-aids unless explicitly requested; if one is unavoidable, state why and mark it with a `TODO` explaining the proper fix.
- Use current, officially supported APIs and idioms for the framework version in use. Do not use deprecated or removed APIs, and do not copy outdated patterns from old tutorials.
- Consult the official documentation for the installed version when unsure. Never guess an API signature or package name; verify it exists.
- Handle edge cases explicitly: null/empty values, network failure, concurrency, large inputs, and permission failures.
- If the best solution is larger or riskier than what was asked, deliver what was asked, and propose the improvement separately instead of silently expanding the scope.

## Self-Verification & Correction
- Before reporting completion, re-read your own diff as a code reviewer would. Check for: unused code, missed edge cases, security issues, leaked resources, and breaking changes.
- Run the project's tests, lint, and build. Never claim something works unless you ran it. If you could not run or verify something, say so explicitly.
- If a command fails, diagnose the root cause before changing code. Do not retry blindly or loop on the same fix. After two failed attempts at the same approach, stop and reassess or ask.
- If you discover your own earlier change was wrong, state it plainly and fix it. Do not patch over it.
- Do not disable tests, lint rules, type checks, or security checks to make an error disappear.
- Finish with a short summary: what changed, why, what was verified, and any remaining risks or assumptions.

## Agent Boundaries
- Ask and wait for confirmation before: deleting files, modifying the database schema (migrations/schema), or making changes that affect more than 5 files.
- Do not modify files outside the scope of the task. Do not refactor unrelated code unless it prevents the execution of the requested task.
- Dependencies: Adhere to the versions specified in the project files (`composer.json`, `pubspec.yaml`, `package.json`, `*.csproj`). Do not add, replace, or upgrade any library without an explicit request. If you find a deprecated or unsupported dependency, do not replace it silently: report it with a recommended alternative and let me decide.
- If a request is ambiguous or has two fundamentally different interpretations, ask before execution.

## Code Quality
- Abstraction: Extract shared code on the third repetition (Rule of Three) and only when the repetition truly represents the same logic. Simple duplication is better than the wrong abstraction.
- Every file, class, or function must have one clear, single responsibility.
- Separate the UI from business logic and the data layer.
- Comments should explain "why" (decisions, constraints, edge cases), not "what" the code does.
- Language: Variable names, functions, and code comments must be in English. Arabic should only be used for UI text and client-facing messages.

## Error Handling & Logging
- Do not swallow errors silently (no empty catch blocks). Handle them or pass them along with clear context.
- Log errors at the appropriate level (error/warning/info) with trace identifiers to aid debugging.
- Do not log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII).
- Do not expose internal technical details (stack traces) to the end user.

## Security
- Treat all inputs (forms, URLs, file paths, external API responses) as untrusted: validate and sanitize them before processing.
- Always use Parameterized Queries or an ORM; never build queries using string concatenation.
- Protect against XSS and CSRF using the framework's default settings, and do not disable them.
- Do not hardcode secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens). Use environment variables, and never commit `.env` files to Git.
- Enforce authorization on the server side for every protected action; never rely on UI hiding alone.

## Performance
- Avoid N+1 queries. Use Pagination or Lazy Loading for long lists (default: 20-50 items per page).
- Do not load large datasets into memory all at once.
- Clean up resources (Listeners, Timers, Streams, Controllers, Subscriptions) when a component is destroyed.
- Add database Indexes for columns used in searching, filtering, and relationships.

## UI/UX
- Responsiveness: Every interface must work on mobile, tablet, and desktop. Do not use fixed dimensions that break layouts.
- Support Light/Dark Mode using centralized color variables instead of hardcoded colors within components.
- Spacing: Use a consistent scale (multiples of 4 or 8).
- Motion: Keep animation durations between 120-250ms. On the web, animate only `transform` and `opacity` unless otherwise justified, and respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Accessibility: Ensure touch targets are at least 44px, maintain sufficient color contrast, and support RTL for Arabic text.

## Testing
- Add a test for every new feature containing logic and for every bug fix (Regression test) using the project's existing testing framework.
- Do not delete or disable a test just to make the build pass.

## Git
- Use Conventional Commits (e.g., `feat: add user login`, `fix: dispose timer in player`).
- Do not create branches, merge, or push without an explicit request.

## Final Rule
The best code is the least and clearest code. Do not add architectural complexity unless solving an actual problem.

# Laravel & PHP Guidelines

- Commands: `php artisan test`, `./vendor/bin/pint` (or the project's designated formatter). If Larastan/PHPStan is installed, also run `./vendor/bin/phpstan analyse`.
- Use the stack already installed in the project (Pest or PHPUnit, Sanctum or Passport, etc.). Do not add or swap packages without explicit approval.
- Target the Laravel and PHP versions in `composer.json`. Use `declare(strict_types=1)`, typed properties, return types, enums, readonly classes, and constructor promotion.

## Architecture (Scalable by Design)
- Request flow: `Route → Middleware → Controller → FormRequest → Action/Service → Model → Resource`.
- Controllers: thin. They only receive the validated request, call one Action/Service, and return a Resource or response. No business logic, queries, or mapping inside Controllers.
- Business logic: one Action class per use case (single `handle`/`execute` method) for isolated operations; Services for related groups of operations.
- Organize by feature/domain as the app grows (e.g. `app/Domain/Orders/{Actions,Models,Enums,DTOs,Events,Policies}`) instead of dumping everything into flat `app/Models` and `app/Services`. Follow the structure already in the project.
- Do not create Repositories by default (Eloquent is the data layer). Introduce one only when there is a real need: multiple data sources, a complex reusable query layer, or required test isolation. Reusable query logic belongs in model scopes or dedicated Query classes.
- Use DTOs (readonly classes) to pass structured data between layers instead of loose arrays.
- Use Enums for fixed sets of values (status, type, role), with Eloquent casts. No magic strings or numbers.
- Use Events/Listeners for side effects that other parts of the system react to. Do not hide core business flow in Observers.
- Inject dependencies through the constructor (service container). Avoid `app()` and static service location inside business classes.
- Keep configuration-driven values in `config/`, not scattered constants.

## Database Access (No Raw SQL)
- Use Eloquent and the Query Builder only. Do not write SQL statements: no `DB::select`, `DB::statement`, `DB::unprepared`, `whereRaw`, `selectRaw`, `orderByRaw`, `havingRaw`, or `DB::raw`.
- The only exception is when Eloquent/Query Builder genuinely cannot express the query (e.g. a database-specific function in a complex report). In that case: always use parameter bindings, never concatenate input, add a comment explaining WHY, and list it in the final summary.
- Migrations use the Schema Builder, not raw SQL.
- Move repeated query conditions into local scopes or custom query builders.
- Use `Model::shouldBeStrict(! app()->isProduction())` (lazy-loading prevention, silently-discarded attributes, and missing-attribute access) in `AppServiceProvider`.
- Avoid N+1 with `with()`/`load()`; use `withCount`/`withSum`/`withExists` instead of loading relations to count them.
- Select only the necessary columns in heavy queries. Always paginate lists (`paginate`/`cursorPaginate`, 20-50 per page). Never call `->get()` or `Model::all()` on unbounded tables.
- Large datasets (>1000 records): `chunkById`, `lazyById`, or `cursor`. Use `chunkById` when updating during iteration.
- Use `exists()` instead of `count() > 0`, and bulk operations (`insert`, `upsert`, `update` on a query) instead of loops of single writes.
- Wrap multi-step writes in `DB::transaction()`. Do not dispatch jobs or events that depend on committed data inside a transaction unless they use `afterCommit`.
- Use `lockForUpdate()` or atomic operations for counters, balances, and stock to avoid race conditions.
- Store money as integers (minor units) or a decimal cast. Never floats.
- Use Soft Deletes only where recovery is a real requirement.

## Migrations & Schema
- Never edit a migration that has already run in a shared environment; create a new one.
- Migrations must be reversible (`down()`), with foreign keys (`constrained()` and an explicit `onDelete` behavior) and indexes on foreign keys and on columns used in `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`, and `JOIN`.
- Use unique constraints at the database level, not only in validation.
- Ask for confirmation before any schema change (consistent with the core rules).

## Validation & Authorization
- Always use Form Requests for validation and `authorize()`. Never put rules inside Controllers.
- Use only `$request->validated()` (or `safe()`). Never `$request->all()` or `$request->input()` blindly into `create`/`update`.
- Define `$fillable` explicitly. Never use `$guarded = []`.
- Authorize every protected action on the server with Policies or Gates (`$this->authorize()`, `can` middleware). Never rely on hiding UI elements.
- Prevent IDOR: always scope queries by the owner/tenant, and use route model binding with scoped bindings.
- Validate file uploads (mimes, max size, dimensions), store them on a non-public disk with generated names, and serve them via signed or authorized routes.

## Security
- Keep CSRF, CORS, and the `web`/`api` middleware defaults enabled. Do not disable them.
- Rate-limit all authentication, OTP, password-reset, and public write endpoints with `RateLimiter`/`throttle`.
- Hash passwords with `Hash` (never custom hashing). Use the `hashed` cast. Regenerate the session on login.
- Escape output with Blade `{{ }}`. Use `{!! !!}` only for sanitized, trusted content.
- Never use `unserialize` on user input. Never use `eval`, `exec`, or `shell_exec` with user data.
- Set security headers and secure cookies in production (`secure`, `httpOnly`, `sameSite`).
- Mask sensitive attributes with `$hidden` and encrypted casts for sensitive fields.
- Never commit `.env`. `APP_DEBUG=false` in production. Debug tools (Telescope, Debugbar) are limited to local/dev.
- Prefer signed URLs for sensitive links, and API tokens with scoped abilities.

## Configuration & Performance
- Call `env()` only inside `config/` files; use `config()` everywhere else.
- Production deployment runs `php artisan optimize` (config, route, event, and view caching) and `composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader`.
- Cache expensive reads with `Cache::remember` using explicit keys and TTLs, and invalidate on write. Do not cache per-user data under shared keys.
- Eager-load only what the response needs. Use API Resources with `whenLoaded` to avoid accidental N+1 in serialization.
- Move slow work (email, notifications, file processing, external API calls, reports) to Queues.

## Queues, Jobs & Scheduling
- Jobs must be idempotent and set `tries`, `backoff`, and `timeout`. Use `ShouldBeUnique` or `WithoutOverlapping` where duplicates are harmful.
- Pass IDs (or `SerializesModels`) to Jobs, not large payloads.
- Implement `failed()` handling and log failures with context.
- Define scheduled tasks in the scheduler (`routes/console.php` or `Kernel`), with `withoutOverlapping()` and `onOneServer()` where relevant.

## API Design
- Use API Resources (`JsonResource`/`ResourceCollection`) for all outputs. Never return Eloquent models directly.
- Version the API (`/api/v1`). Use consistent HTTP status codes and one standard error format.
- Use route groups, route model binding, and resource controllers. Keep routes files free of logic (no closures in production routes, so route caching works).
- Return paginated, filterable collections with a documented query contract.

## Error Handling & Logging
- Handle exceptions centrally in `bootstrap/app.php` (or `app/Exceptions/Handler.php`). Create domain exceptions for business rule violations and map them to proper responses.
- Do not swallow exceptions. Do not expose stack traces or internal messages to clients in production.
- Log with proper levels and context (user ID, request ID). Never log passwords, tokens, or PII.
- Use `report()` for handled-but-notable errors.

## Localization & Frontend Contract
- User-facing strings go in `lang/` files (Arabic and English as needed), not hardcoded in PHP or Blade. Validation messages go through the translation system.
- Return consistent, translatable error messages from the API, plus machine-readable error codes.

## Testing
- Feature tests for every endpoint (success, validation failure, unauthorized, forbidden). Unit tests for Actions/Services.
- Use factories and `RefreshDatabase` (or the project's equivalent). No dependency on seeded production-like data.
- Add a test asserting no N+1 for critical list endpoints (query count assertion or lazy-loading prevention).
- Fake external services (`Http::fake`, `Queue::fake`, `Mail::fake`, `Event::fake`). Never call real external APIs from tests.
- Add a regression test for every bug fix. Never disable a test to make the build pass.
